Z||000011||mrfiero||08-13-2000||03:33 AM||||I'm back!!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Actually, I've been back since Thursday (I've posted a few things already).  I want to thank everyone for their concern for me....it's nice to know people care.  <P>This is sort of long, but here is what happened after I saw RossT around May 20th.....<P>I took a road trip to Maine to drop off a bunch of large Fiero items to Tim Perrigo.  He had bought some things off of me and it was cheaper to drive there than ship it, plus I had bought an '84 coupe off of an eBay auction.  It was in Detroit, so I planned on picking it up on the way home.<P>The trip was pretty uneventful, until I entered Iowa.  Evidently my catalytic convertor decided to plug itself up.  The result was 8-10 MPG for the last 1000 miles of my trip (I was getting about 15 MPG before).  I was filling up every 100-110 miles just when the gas prices exploded.  I believe the highest I paid on that trip was $1.90/gal.<P>I pulled into North Platte, Nebraska around 10 pm to fill up the tank and grab a bite to eat and wound up getting into an accident.  There were 2 sets of traffic lights , since there it was a divided road.....I didn't see the first set and drove right through a red light.  I clipped the rear end of a 1990 Chevy pickup.  It ripped the right headlight assembly and door right off my GT, along with the front half of the fender and the corner of the hood.  Luckily the only damage to the truck was a bent bumper.<P>The cops came and filled out a report.....I paid for the guys bumper and never got a ticket (thank goodness!).  It took a couple of hours to make the necessary repairs (pretty easy when you're hauling another Fiero!) and I filled up the tank and was about to head out again when another cop pulled up.  I thought he had changed his mind about writing me a ticket, but it was someone else instead.  This officer owned an '86 GT and was just stopping by to say hi!   He had no idea I was just involved in an accident.  We spoke for awhile and exchanged names and what not, then I left.<P>I made it home on May 28th and relaxed.  Here is where the story gets interesting......<P>It starts in Feb. 1999.  I was on my way home from The Fiero Factory in Alabama (I had just quit and was on my way back to Colorado).  I was driving an '87 coupe that was literally built a few days earlier.  I had blown the engine in my turbo quad-4 '85 GT and sold it to Jeremy (mechanic at Ed's).  He, in return, gave me this '87 coupe.  We put in another engine and I was off.  I was about 50 miles from Denver (around 11pm) when I was pulled over by a state trooper.  I wasn't speeding (70 in a 75 zone), so I had no idea why he stopped me.  Evidently the passenger side headlight motor pooped out on me and was only 3/4 of the way up.  He then asked me for my license, registration and insurance....this is where it gets ugly.....since Jeremy had never planned on ever driving this car again, he never got the title in his name.  Also, since we just built the thing prior to my departure it wasn't registered (I put on Jeremy's Florida plates from his Fiero).....and of course I hadn't gotten insurance on it either.  This alone was bad enough, but it got worse.....evidently my license had expired while I was in Alabama.  Add everything together and it spelled trouble.  I, with no drivers license, was driving a car that wasn't registered or licensed and had no insurance.  I couldn't prove that it was even my car! <P>I'm very surprised I wasn't arrested on the spot, but instead the trooper just gave me a ticket.  I kept putting it off and putting it off, but I finally had to go to court to get it behind me.  My court date was on May 30th...just a few days after I returned from my trip.  I fully expected to spend 30 days in jail (which is why I mentioned that I might be gone for a month).  I about choked when the judge said 120 days in jail, but I somehow kept my composure.  I spent 7 days in county jail before being transferred to a work release program.  Since selling Fiero stuff isn't a "real" job (I have no physical business address or licenses, etc) I wasn't able to get anything done.  I had to take a job at a Domino's Pizza (my friend is the manager) just to stay in the program.  I served 72 days of my sentence and was released on August 10th (a week after my birthday).<P>That, my friends, is what happened to me and why I seemingly fell off the face of the Earth.  I'm back, a little humbled, but no worse for wear.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-13-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000012||Gokart||08-13-2000||06:56 AM||||Sorry that happened to you mrfiero. Hope your luck changes and at least it's in the PAST now and you can move on. Sometimes ****  happens to even good people, but those are the ones that learn from the things that happen and move on! Unfortunatly there's alot of people out there committing awful crimes and get off scott free <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> I hope a serious string of good fortune comes your way <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gokart_sig_1.gif"> <P>Marc                     87GT||||reg||9
Z||000013||Monkeyman||08-13-2000||12:09 PM||||Sorry to hear about your unfortunate mishap with the cops.  Why did you quit working for Ed??  If this is a personal thing, forget I asked, but if this has to do with the Fiero Factory, I'd like to know as I had planned on taking my car down there next summer for a 3.6/GX3.||||reg||1
Z||000014||87ECO5||08-13-2000||02:26 PM||||I'm really sorry to hear about this, Mike, but I'm glad you're back - AND SAFE!  We were worried about you.||||reg||14
Z||000015||mrfiero||08-13-2000||04:59 PM||||Gokart,<P>Thanks.  I'll be going to Vegas in a few weeks, so hopefully that good luck will kick in about that time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>Monkeyman,  <P>It really had nothing to do with Ed or the Fiero Factory......I was having problems with my girlfriend (now ex-girlfriend) and that put me in a bad frame of mind.  I needed to just get away from things for a while and regroup.  <P>I would not hesitate to let Ed, Dave or Jeremy work on my car.....they know their stuff!   <P>87ECO5,<P>Thanks.....it's nice to be back too!<P><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000016||LarryB||08-13-2000||09:47 PM||||Welcome back Mike...glad to hear that you're OK and in 1 piece. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||SCCA FIERO||08-13-2000||10:05 PM||||WOW!! That sounds like a story that you would only read on the internet that happens to other people  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Mrfiero, I think a while back you said you may have some brakets to hold the grills on an 88GT. I think it was you (before your little encounter), but if I'm wrong then just ignore this.<P>Bet you never thought something like that could happen to you, huh?<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR><B>KEEP THE EXCITEMENT ALIVE!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000018||mrfiero||08-14-2000||12:28 AM||||I think I do have those brackets.....give me a few days to dig them up.<P>I can honestly say that I had no idea that would happen.  It kind of ticks me off that the only reason I was pulled over for was a faulty headlight (it was on and I could see just fine).  Oh well.....live and learn.||||reg||10
